# Build artifacts that `git add -A` will otherwise sweep into a commit. This has
# happened twice on this branch, once pulling in an agent's in-progress files and
# once a directory of build output. Stage by explicit path; these are the backstop.
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Eval transcripts are COMMITTED. This rule used to ignore evals/runs/**/q*.jsonl
# and it cost us a run.
#
# The pattern only matched q*, so the cheap bobby-lanes transcripts (b*) were
# tracked while the expensive base-matrix ones (q*) were not -- exactly backwards.
# On 17 Sep a re-run that died during setup overwrote fifteen completed
# argo/claudesonnet45 transcripts, about 31.8k output tokens over 2.29M input
# tokens of gateway spend, and there was no copy anywhere: not in git because of
# this line, and not in any archive. Among them were the six silent empties that
# were the raw evidence for how that fault was characterised.
#
# The whole tree is 7.3 MB over 386 files. That is nothing against losing a run,
# and these transcripts ARE the evidence for every number we quote -- an eval
# result with no transcript behind it is an assertion.
#
# If the tree ever does get large, archive old runs out of it deliberately rather
# than ignoring them silently. RUNS.md records every run either way.

# Scratch patch scripts. How a change was made, not what the change is -- the
# commit message is the durable record. Committed once by accident on 17 Sep,
# in the very commit whose README says they are never committed.
evals/_patch*.py
_patch*.py

# Anything that looks like an env file, in any spelling. `*.env` does NOT match
# `.env.bak`, `.env.env.bak-153724` or `.env.local` -- a backup of a credentials
# file written during an edit on 17 Sep was committable for about a minute
# because of exactly that gap.
.env
.env.*
*.env
*.env.*
!.env.example
